{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And it became a serpent, etc.</b> The intention of this sign is to allude that the dimension of the kelipah is associated with the serpent. Go and learn from the primordial serpent, for it is the aspect of Samekh-Mem, and He alluded to him that through the instrumentality of Moses' hands, with their mighty strength, the power of the serpent is nullified and it becomes dry wood; and when he relaxes his hand from it and casts it down, it becomes a serpent — to the point that Moses fled from it. Or perhaps this is what He intended in saying <b>what is this in your hand</b> — meaning, that he should perceive a subtle insight in it: if it began to move like the desert mouse, it would begin to come alive little by little. And sometimes one finds a tiny fraction of it as a mouse, and sometimes a small part, and sometimes half of it. And Moses answered <b>a staff</b> — meaning, it is entirely dry wood; <b>and he cast it to the ground and it became a serpent</b> — meaning, it became one instantaneously: in a single moment it was a serpent." "And the verse's saying <b>to the ground</b> alludes that in the estimation of the serpent, the greatness of the earth is magnified, for it is his food and is reckoned to him (Genesis 3:14), and He showed Moses many things through this, and also that he should perform the sign itself for Israel." "And the verse's saying <b>send forth your hand</b>, etc. — meaning, He showed him that even though it had left his domain, there is power in Moses to seize hold of it and strip it of its strength." "And the verse's saying <b>and grasp it by its tail</b>, and not saying 'it' [i.e., 'grasp it'], HaShem intended to tell him: since your fear of the serpent applies if you take hold of it by its tail — for if it were clever enough to seize its head, the biter would feel nothing from the entire tail — therefore HaShem said to him: grasp it even by its tail, without fear. And likewise it says <b>and he seized it</b> — meaning, in whatever place chance presented it to him, without fear, and it became a staff in his grasp <b>in his palm</b> — meaning, through the touch of Moses' palm its vitality dried up at once and no life-force remained in it at all, and this is what the verse says: ״וַיְהִי לְמַטֶּה בְּכַפּוֹ״."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:22.084213941Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}